# Conventional Commits for convco: which commit types move the version, and how the changelog names them. `convco check` fails a commit whose type is not listed here, `convco version --bump` reads `increment`, and `convco changelog` reads `section` and `hidden` to write the release notes, which have no heading of their own because GitHub shows the release title above them.
#
# From 1.0.0, a feat commit bumps the minor version, fix, perf, revert, refactor, docs and build commits bump the patch version, and a breaking change (a `!` after the type or a BREAKING CHANGE footer) bumps the major version. Below 1.0.0 convco follows Cargo's convention that a 0.x minor version is the breaking one: a breaking change bumps the minor version and every other releasing commit, feat included, bumps the patch version. ci, test, style and chore commits release nothing on their own.
header: ""
types:
  - {type: feat, increment: Minor, section: Features, hidden: false}
  - {type: fix, increment: Patch, section: Bug Fixes, hidden: false}
  - {type: perf, increment: Patch, section: Performance Improvements, hidden: false}
  - {type: revert, increment: Patch, section: Reverts, hidden: false}
  - {type: refactor, increment: Patch, section: Code Refactoring, hidden: false}
  - {type: docs, increment: Patch, section: Documentation, hidden: false}
  - {type: build, increment: Patch, section: Build System, hidden: false}
  - {type: ci, increment: None, section: Continuous Integration, hidden: false}
  - {type: test, increment: None, section: Tests, hidden: false}
  - {type: style, increment: None, section: Styles, hidden: false}
  - {type: chore, increment: None, section: Other, hidden: true}
